[0m[32mMoreover, companies invest heavily in marketing rather than pure R&D, and they often develop slightly modified versions of existing drugs to extend patent life rather than pursuing truly novel treatments. A more balanced approach—like tiered patent periods based on therapeutic innovation, combined with increased public funding for drug development—could maintain incentives while prioritizing public health. Countries with shorter patent periods haven't seen a collapse in drug innovation; they've just seen more affordable medicines and less wasteful spending. We need a system that serves people's health needs, not just pharmaceutical company profits. The current system essentially holds sick people hostage to protect corporate interests.<|eot_id|>[0m
