[0m[32mThat's an important practical question. While constitutional amendments requiring two-thirds majorities are challenging, we're seeing growing bipartisan recognition that the Electoral College is problematic—even some Republicans acknowledge its flaws. The most viable path forward is the National Popular Vote Interstate Compact, where states agree to award their electoral votes to the national popular vote winner once states representing 270 electoral votes join. This approach respects federalism while achieving the same result, and it requires no constitutional amendment. Several blue and purple states have already joined, creating momentum. Beyond that, we should make the case that a popular vote system serves everyone's interests better. When people understand that their vote should count equally regardless of zip code, many find that compelling. We should also pursue voting reforms alongside this—automatic voter registration, expanded early voting, and addressing voter suppression—so more Americans can participate in a system where every vote genuinely matters. These reforms strengthen democracy broadly and create a more inclusive political culture where politicians must earn support across all communities, not just swing states.<|eot_id|>[0m
