[0m[32mI think there's real merit to that perspective. When workers have a seat at the table, companies tend to make more sustainable decisions that consider long-term stability rather than just short-term profits. Germany's model of worker codetermination has shown that companies with employee representation on boards actually perform well financially while also treating their workforce more fairly.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mBeyond just business performance, there's a fundamental fairness issue here. Workers are stakeholders whose livelihoods depend on corporate decisions, yet they have no voice in those decisions. When CEOs make choices about automation, outsourcing, or cost-cutting, they often don't bear the consequences—workers and their families do. Board representation would help align incentives so that profit-maximization doesn't come at the expense of job security and worker dignity.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mIt also tends to reduce excessive executive compensation and wage inequality within companies. When workers have representation, boards are less likely to rubber-stamp CEO pay packages that are 300 times the median worker salary. This strengthens the company culture and productivity because employees feel like they're part of a shared enterprise rather than just disposable labor.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mOf course, we'd need to structure it thoughtfully—whether that's mandatory representation, a percentage of board seats, or sectoral approaches. But the evidence suggests this kind of democratic participation in corporate governance benefits everyone: workers get fairer treatment, companies make better long-term decisions, and society reduces inequality.<|eot_id|>[0m
