[0m[33m
[0m[32mWhile I understand the intent behind occupational licensing—ensuring quality and safety—the evidence shows it often functions more as a barrier to entry that protects existing workers rather than consumers. Licensing requirements have expanded dramatically and frequently have little correlation with public safety. For example, some states require licenses for professions with minimal risk of harm.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mThe real issue is that licensing can trap low-income workers in poverty by creating expensive credentialing hurdles, disproportionately affecting communities of color who have historically faced barriers to professional advancement. This reduces economic mobility and workforce diversity.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mA better approach would be more transparent, evidence-based standards that genuinely protect consumers without unnecessary restrictions. We should invest in robust enforcement of workplace safety regulations, consumer protection laws, and accessible complaint mechanisms instead. We can also support portable certifications and reciprocal licensing agreements across states to reduce bureaucratic burden on workers.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mThe goal should be worker dignity and economic opportunity alongside genuine consumer protection—not using licensing as a way to restrict competition and keep wages artificially high for those already in the profession.<|eot_id|>[0m
