[0m[32mI think there's a strong case for it. These tech giants have accumulated unprecedented power over our digital infrastructure and public discourse. Companies like Meta and Google control how billions of people access information, which gives them enormous influence over what we see and believe. When a handful of corporations can shape political narratives and suppress competition, it undermines democracy itself.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mBreaking them up would serve multiple progressive goals simultaneously. It would protect workers by reducing corporate concentration and restoring bargaining power. It would help small businesses and startups compete fairly instead of being crushed or acquired. It would give us better privacy protections and algorithmic accountability that these monopolies have consistently resisted.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mThere's also the environmental angle—these companies' massive data centers consume enormous amounts of energy. Competition might drive more sustainable practices.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mThe argument that "breaking them up would hurt innovation" doesn't hold up. We had incredible innovation before tech monopolies formed, and smaller competitors are often more agile and creative. What we need is regulation that prevents this concentration from happening again—stronger antitrust enforcement, mandatory interoperability standards, and real consequences for anticompetitive behavior.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mThis isn't anti-business; it's pro-competition and pro-democracy. We regulated railroads and utilities the same way. Tech companies shouldn't be exempt just because they call themselves innovative.<|eot_id|>[0m
