[0m[33mSchool voucher programs improve education through competition<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[32mI understand the appeal of that argument, but the evidence tells a different story. Voucher programs actually tend to drain resources from public schools, which serve the vast majority of students—particularly low-income families and students with disabilities who are harder and more expensive to educate. When we divert public funding to private schools through vouchers, we're essentially creating a two-tiered system that leaves behind the most vulnerable students. Public schools need stable, adequate funding to thrive, not competition that fragments resources. Countries with the strongest education systems, like Finland and Singapore, invest heavily in robust public education for all students rather than creating parallel private systems. We should focus on fully funding our public schools, reducing class sizes, supporting teachers, and ensuring equitable resources across districts instead of undermining the system that educates 90% of American children.<|eot_id|>[0m[33m<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[33mBut wouldn't competition force public schools to improve or lose students and funding?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[32mThat market-based logic sounds reasonable on the surface, but it doesn't work well for essential public services like education. The problem is that competition in education isn't like competition in the marketplace for consumer goods—it actually increases inequality. Students whose parents have information, transportation, and means to navigate the voucher system gain advantages, while disadvantaged families remain in underfunded public schools. And when you remove engaged families and funding from public schools, you create a downward spiral: fewer resources, larger class sizes, harder to attract quality teachers, which drives away more families if they can afford to leave. We've seen this play out in cities like New Orleans and Indianapolis. Instead of betting on competition, we should strengthen public schools universally. Higher teacher salaries, modern facilities, smaller classes, comprehensive support services—these proven investments benefit all children regardless of zip code. Education isn't a consumer product; it's a public good that strengthens our entire democracy. Our focus should be lifting up the whole system, not creating winners and losers.<|eot_id|>[0m
