[0m[32mI appreciate the nuance in your framing, but I have some significant concerns with this approach. While I strongly support students' individual right to pray privately or form voluntary religious clubs, allowing student-led prayer during school time or at school events creates real problems.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mFirst, even student-led prayer in a public school setting can pressure students of minority faiths or no faith to participate or feel excluded. Public schools serve all families regardless of their religious beliefs, and they should be neutral spaces where every student feels equally welcome and included.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mSecond, "student-led" doesn't eliminate the school's endorsement—the institution is still facilitating it on school grounds and time. This blurs the line between protecting religious freedom and the school promoting religion, which historically has disadvantaged minority communities.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mWhat we should absolutely protect: students forming prayer clubs, having moments of personal reflection, wearing religious symbols, and practicing their faith outside of official school events. That's genuine religious freedom.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mWhat public schools should remain neutral on: official or semi-official prayer during school time, at assemblies, or before events. This approach actually strengthens religious liberty for everyone by keeping government out of religion entirely.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mWe can support both student religious expression AND keep our public institutions secular and inclusive. These aren't in conflict when we protect private religious practice while maintaining institutional neutrality.<|eot_id|>[0m
