[0m[32mWhile I understand that argument, we need to look at the real-world consequences. Yes, patents can spur some innovation, but they also create massive barriers to access. Right now, people are rationing insulin and skipping doses because of patent-protected pricing. We could maintain innovation incentives through other means—like direct government funding for research, which has actually produced many of our most important drugs. Countries with stronger price regulations still see pharmaceutical innovation. The current system prioritizes corporate profits over human lives, and that's a moral failure we need to address through stronger government negotiation powers and price controls.<|eot_id|>[0m[33m<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>
