[0m[33mBut wouldn't price controls discourage companies from investing in developing drugs for rare diseases?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[32mThat's a fair concern, but it assumes the current patent system is the only solution. First, we already have mechanisms like orphan drug designations that provide incentives for rare disease research without relying solely on extreme price gouging. Second, public investment and government-funded research institutions could take on more of that burden—the NIH already funds billions in basic research that companies build on. We could also use tiered pricing models where wealthy nations subsidize medications for developing countries. The fundamental issue is that treating essential medicines as pure profit centers—where companies charge what the market will bear rather than what people can afford—is incompatible with a just healthcare system. Innovation matters, but so does ensuring people don't die because they can't afford life-saving drugs. We need to fundamentally restructure how we balance those priorities in favor of collective human welfare.<|eot_id|>[0m
