[0m[32mI understand that concern, but I'd frame it differently. Sanctuary cities aren't trying to undermine federal law—they're exercising local discretion about how to allocate their limited police resources. These cities have found that when immigrants fear deportation, they won't report crimes, making communities less safe overall. By not detaining people solely for immigration status, these cities can focus on actual public safety. It's also about dignity and due process. We're talking about real people—neighbors, workers, families—who contribute to their communities. A humane immigration system should prioritize legal pathways and integration, not mass deportations that tear families apart and hurt labor-dependent industries. The real issue is Congress's failure to pass comprehensive immigration reform.<|eot_id|>[0m[33m<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[33mBut doesn't this create a two-tiered system where some cities follow federal law and others don't?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[32mThat's a fair question about consistency, but it's important to distinguish between law-breaking and policy disagreement. Sanctuary cities aren't breaking federal law—ICE can still arrest and deport people. These cities simply choose not to use their local police as federal immigration agents. That's actually appropriate federalism: local governments responding to their constituents' needs. And yes, there's variation in how cities handle this, but that's true across many policy areas—it's part of how our system works. What we really need is federal immigration reform that creates pathways to citizenship for undocumented immigrants already here, strengthens worker protections so employers can't exploit migrant labor, and ensures asylum seekers get fair hearings. Until Congress acts, sanctuary policies acknowledge reality: millions of people are here, contributing to our economy, and deserve to live without constant fear. That's both pragmatic and ethical.<|eot_id|>[0m
