[0m[32mConsider other public health requirements we already accept without much controversy—food safety standards, water sanitation, seat belt laws. These exist because individual choices can harm others. During disease outbreaks, unvaccinated people become vectors that endanger the broader community.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mI'd also note that no right is absolute. We restrict freedom of speech when it causes direct harm, we restrict movement to prevent crime. Similarly, vaccine requirements during serious health crises balance individual liberty with collective welfare—especially when safe, effective vaccines are available at no cost.
