[0m[32mI appreciate you raising this, but I think we need to reframe how we approach immigration. Border security and creating pathways to citizenship aren't mutually exclusive—in fact, they work better together.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mWhen we have millions of undocumented workers, they become vulnerable to exploitation. They're paid substandard wages, work in unsafe conditions, and can't report labor violations without risking deportation. This actually depresses wages for all workers. A legalization program would bring people into the formal economy, allowing enforcement of labor standards that protect everyone.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mWe also need to remember the human element here. Most immigrants come seeking opportunity and fleeing hardship. They contribute to our communities—as workers, entrepreneurs, and neighbors. Rather than spending billions on enforcement and deportation, which separates families and creates humanitarian crises, we could invest in processing immigration claims efficiently and humanely.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mEffective border management means modern infrastructure, technology, and personnel—which we can afford. But we should couple that with practical solutions like work visas tied to labor market needs and pathways for long-term residents to earn citizenship. Countries like Canada show you can have both secure borders and progressive immigration policies.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mThe real question is: what kind of society do we want to be? One that respects human dignity and creates broad-based prosperity, or one that relies on an exploited underclass while spending enormous resources on enforcement theater?<|eot_id|>[0m
