[0m[33m
[0m[33mInheritance taxes are double taxation and should be eliminated<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[32mI understand that concern about double taxation, but I'd respectfully push back on that framing. Inheritance taxes aren't really taxing the same income twice—they're taxing a transfer of wealth that happens to be unearned by the recipient. The original earner already paid income tax on that money when they made it. But when that wealth transfers to the next generation without any work or contribution from them, that's a separate economic event that we can reasonably tax to fund public goods and reduce wealth concentration.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mMore fundamentally, extreme wealth concentration undermines equal opportunity. When billionaires can pass vast fortunes to their children untaxed, we're essentially creating dynasties where success depends on who your parents are, not your talent or effort. Reasonable estate taxes help level the playing field and fund investments in education, infrastructure, and healthcare that benefit everyone. Studies show that most families—over 99%—don't pay federal estate taxes anyway because of high exemption thresholds. So we're really talking about preventing a small elite from accumulating unprecedented dynastic power.<|eot_id|>[0m[33m<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[33mBut shouldn't people have the right to leave what they've earned to their children without government taking a cut?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[32mThat's a fair sentiment, and I think most people do want to provide for their families—that's natural and important. But we also have to ask: what rights do we prioritize when they conflict?
[0m[32m
[0m[32mConsider it this way: your right to leave money to your kids exists within a society that provided infrastructure, legal protections, educated workers, and public safety that enabled you to earn that wealth in the first place. There's a legitimate public interest in how concentrated wealth gets passed down, especially when it can translate into outsized political influence and reduced opportunity for others.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mAlso, let's be practical about what we're discussing. A parent can currently pass $13.61 million per person to heirs without any federal estate tax—that's substantial. The tax only applies to extraordinary wealth concentration. Most middle-class families transfer everything tax-free. Nobody's arguing we should prevent people from leaving their homes or modest savings to their children.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mBut when wealth becomes so concentrated that it shapes policy and limits opportunity for the broader population, that's when government has a role to act in the public interest. The revenue from estate taxes could fund education, job training, and infrastructure—investments that actually create opportunity for the next generation across all economic backgrounds, not just the wealthy ones. That seems like a reasonable trade-off to me.<|eot_id|>[0m
