[0m[32mI respectfully disagree. While I understand the market efficiency argument, we have historical evidence showing that without a minimum wage floor, workers—particularly the most vulnerable—face exploitation and poverty wages. Before minimum wage laws, we saw documented cases of employers paying subsistence-level wages because workers had limited bargaining power, especially during economic downturns. A fair market doesn't exist in a vacuum; there's an inherent power imbalance between individual workers and employers with capital. The minimum wage isn't about market interference—it's about establishing basic human dignity standards. Studies show that modest minimum wage increases don't significantly harm employment and actually stimulate the economy because low-wage workers spend additional income immediately. Plus, when workers earn livable wages, we reduce reliance on public assistance programs, which ultimately saves taxpayers money. The goal should be ensuring everyone who works full-time can afford housing, food, and healthcare. That's not anti-market; that's pro-worker and pro-society.<|eot_id|>[0m
