[0m[32mI appreciate the sentiment, but the evidence on school choice is more nuanced than that. While choice sounds appealing, market-based education models often deepen inequality. When families with resources cherry-pick schools, it leaves under-resourced students concentrated in struggling schools. Studies show that robust funding for all public schools and equitable resource distribution are stronger predictors of student success than choice alone. We should focus on making every neighborhood school excellent, ensuring every child—regardless of zip code or family income—has access to quality education, experienced teachers, and proper facilities. That's how we truly empower families and improve outcomes for everyone.<|eot_id|>[0m[33m<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>
