[0m[33m
[0m[32mThat's a fair question, but the data shows a more complicated picture. While some individual charter schools perform well, the charter sector overall shows mixed results—many underperform traditional public schools, particularly in math and reading. More importantly, charter expansion diverts funding and engaged families from public schools, which still serve 90% of students. This creates a two-tiered system that harms the students left behind. Instead of splitting resources, we should invest heavily in strengthening public education for all: competitive teacher salaries, modern facilities, mental health services, and smaller class sizes. Countries with the strongest education systems—Finland, South Korea—don't rely on choice and competition. They fund schools equitably and trust professional educators. We can do the same here by treating education as a shared public good rather than a consumer product.<|eot_id|>[0m
