[0m[33m
[0m[32mI understand that perspective, but I'd respectfully push back. Right-to-work laws actually undermine workers' collective power and have been shown to lower wages and benefits across entire industries. When workers can opt out of union membership while still benefiting from union-negotiated contracts, it weakens the financial sustainability of unions that fight for better working conditions for everyone. Research consistently shows that right-to-work states have lower average wages and worse workplace safety records. True worker freedom means having a strong voice through collective bargaining—the ability to organize together for fair pay, benefits, and safe conditions. Without that collective power, individual workers face enormous imbalances in negotiating with large employers. We should be strengthening workers' rights to organize, not making it easier for employers to fragment the workforce.<|eot_id|>[0m[33m<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[33mBut don't unions sometimes protect bad workers and prevent merit-based advancement?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[32mThat's a common criticism, but it oversimplifies how unions actually function. Yes, unions protect due process—but that's a feature, not a bug. Without union protections, employers could fire workers arbitrarily, retaliate against those who speak up about safety issues, or show favoritism. Seniority systems, while imperfect, ensure fairness and predictability that benefit workers generally. On merit: unions absolutely allow for advancement based on qualifications and performance within their frameworks. The real issue is that without unions, "merit" often becomes code for discrimination, connections, or employer whim. Studies show union shops actually have better documented performance metrics and clearer advancement criteria than non-union workplaces. The bigger picture is worker dignity and security—knowing you have rights and protections so you're not constantly vulnerable to being replaced or punished for organizing. That stability actually helps both workers and businesses in the long run through reduced turnover and better morale.<|eot_id|>[0m
