[0m[32mI understand that's how the Supreme Court has ruled in recent decisions, but I think we need to look at this more carefully in the context of public health and collective welfare. The Second Amendment was written in 1791 when muskets took minutes to reload—the framers couldn't have imagined modern semi-automatic weapons. Other wealthy democracies have strong gun ownership traditions but implement reasonable regulations like background checks, waiting periods, and restrictions on high-capacity magazines, and they have dramatically lower rates of gun violence and mass shootings. We can respect the legitimate right to self-defense and hunting while also acknowledging that common-sense regulations protect everyone's right to safety and life. Countries like Canada and Australia have done this successfully. I think we should focus on what policies actually save lives—universal background checks have broad public support, including among gun owners—rather than treating any regulation as an infringement. Our kids deserve to feel safe in schools, and workers deserve safe communities. That's what I believe should guide this conversation.<|eot_id|>[0m
