[0m[32mI completely agree. Healthcare is a fundamental human right, not a luxury commodity that should only be accessible to those with wealth. Every person deserves access to medical care regardless of their ability to pay. When we look at countries with universal healthcare systems—like Canada, Germany, and the Nordic nations—we see that government-funded healthcare actually delivers better health outcomes at lower costs per capita than our current fragmented, profit-driven system. In the United States, medical bankruptcy is a leading cause of personal financial ruin, and people routinely skip medications or delay treatment because of cost. This isn't just morally wrong; it's economically inefficient. A healthier population is more productive, misses less work, and contributes more to society. We should be investing in preventive care and treating healthcare as a public good, similar to education and infrastructure. When healthcare is tied to employment, it traps people in jobs they might otherwise leave, stifles entrepreneurship, and places an unfair burden on workers and businesses. A government-funded system would free up resources for innovation, reduce administrative waste, and ensure that coverage isn't lost due to job changes or unemployment. This is about building a more equitable society where every person has dignity and access to the care they need.<|eot_id|>[0m
