[0m[33m
[0m[32mI appreciate the appeal of that argument, but I think voucher programs actually undermine our public education system when we need to strengthen it most. Here's my concern: when we redirect public funding to private schools through vouchers, we're essentially draining resources from the students who need them most—kids in under-resourced communities who can't afford private school tuition even with a voucher. Public schools serve everyone regardless of ability to pay, and that's a fundamental democratic principle. Rather than fragmenting education into haves and have-nots, we should be investing robustly in our public schools—better teacher pay, smaller class sizes, updated facilities. That creates real choice and opportunity for all families, not just those who can supplement vouchers with their own wealth.<|eot_id|>[0m[33m<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[33mBut don't wealthy families already have choices through private schools? Why shouldn't working families have that option too?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[32mThat's a fair question, and I get why it seems like leveling the playing field. But here's the practical problem: voucher amounts rarely cover full private school tuition, so they mostly benefit families who already have some financial cushion to top up the difference. Meanwhile, the public school serving a low-income neighborhood loses that funding, which often means cutting programs, larger classes, and reduced teacher pay—making conditions worse for the kids left behind. It's not actually expanding access; it's creating a tiered system. What would genuinely give working families choice is fully funding public schools so they're excellent everywhere—new buildings, experienced teachers, arts and sports programs, counselors. A kid in rural Mississippi or urban Detroit should have access to the same quality education as a kid in the suburbs. That's the kind of real choice that benefits everyone. Universal excellence beats selective escape routes.<|eot_id|>[0m
