[0m[32mI completely agree with this perspective. Single-family zoning is a major barrier to housing affordability and has deep roots in exclusionary practices that have perpetuated racial and economic segregation in our communities.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mWhen cities eliminate restrictive zoning, we open up opportunities for diverse housing types—duplexes, townhouses, small apartment buildings—that can provide more affordable options for working families, young people, and seniors on fixed incomes. This is especially critical given our affordable housing crisis.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mBeyond housing economics, zoned single-family neighborhoods often consume more land and infrastructure per capita, which increases environmental costs and makes public transit less viable. Mixed-use, denser neighborhoods are actually more sustainable and create more vibrant communities with better walkability.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mIt's also worth noting that single-family zoning historically was weaponized to enforce segregation and keep out lower-income residents and communities of color. Reforming these policies is about correcting that injustice and building more equitable cities where people of all income levels can afford to live and build lives.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mOf course, this needs to be paired with strong tenant protections, community input processes, and ensuring that development benefits longtime residents rather than just enriching developers. But fundamentally, restricting housing supply while so many struggle to afford rent is a policy choice that perpetuates inequality. We can do better.<|eot_id|>[0m
