[0m[32mYou're touching on a genuinely concerning issue that deserves more public attention. When a private individual can unilaterally control critical infrastructure that governments and citizens depend on, we're essentially outsourcing national security to corporate interests. This is particularly troubling given Musk's unpredictable track record and his willingness to weaponize his platforms for political purposes.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mThe broader problem is that we've allowed essential services—communication networks, energy infrastructure, transportation—to become concentrated in the hands of billionaire entrepreneurs without adequate public oversight. A democratic society needs these systems to be accountable to the people, not subject to the whims of one person's ego or business interests. We need stronger regulations requiring that critical infrastructure has meaningful government participation and transparent governance structures. This isn't about stifling innovation; it's about ensuring that vital services serve the collective welfare rather than individual profit motives.<|eot_id|>[0m[33m<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>
