[0m[33mBut wouldn't that be unfair to people who already paid off their loans or didn't go to college?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[32mThat's a fair question, and I understand the concern about fairness. But I'd frame it differently: we're not punishing anyone who paid off their loans—we're finally helping people who are struggling now. We make collective investments all the time that benefit some groups more than others: tax breaks for homeowners, infrastructure projects, farm subsidies. The question is whether education access is important enough to prioritize, and I believe it is. That said, this shouldn't be our only education investment. We should also make public college tuition-free or much more affordable going forward, expand vocational training programs, and increase support for trade schools. This way we're addressing the root problem while also helping current borrowers. Regarding those who paid their way through—many of them also struggled, and ideally they would've had more support too. But the reality is we have a crisis now affecting millions. We can acknowledge that the system was unfair to everyone while still taking action to help people currently in debt. It's about collective progress.<|eot_id|>[0m
