[0m[33mSchool choice vouchers improve educational outcomes for low-income students<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[32mI understand the appeal of that argument, but the evidence actually tells a more complicated story. While vouchers sound empowering in theory, research shows they often drain resources from already under-funded public schools that serve the most vulnerable students. When families with means leave public systems, they take funding with them, leaving behind students with greater needs and fewer resources. Studies from Indiana, Louisiana, and Washington D.C. show voucher programs haven't improved outcomes for low-income students compared to well-funded public schools. Instead of fragmenting education, we should invest robustly in public schools—smaller class sizes, better teacher pay, modern facilities, and comprehensive support services. That's what actually lifts all students up, especially those facing economic hardship.<|eot_id|>[0m[33m<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[33mBut don't vouchers give low-income families the same choices wealthy families already have?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[32mThat's a fair question on the surface, but it misses a crucial reality: choice only works if there are genuinely good options available. Wealthy families don't just get choice—they get choice backed by resources. They can afford to live in good school districts, hire tutors, or pay for private school directly. Low-income families? Vouchers rarely cover full private school tuition, so they're still subsidizing the difference. And private schools can be selective—they're not required to take every student. So vulnerable kids with learning disabilities, behavioral challenges, or language barriers often end up back in underfunded public schools anyway. Real choice means strengthening the schools that serve everyone—the ones that can't turn anyone away. That requires public investment, smaller class sizes, counselors, special education services. When we divide our resources and attention, we inevitably leave the most disadvantaged behind. True equity means everyone gets access to excellent public schools, not a lottery system where some escape while others struggle.<|eot_id|>[0m
